{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The dynamic memory network (DMN) obtained high accuracy on a variety of language tasks.","source_span":"One such architecture, the dynamic memory network (DMN), obtained high accuracy on a variety of language tasks."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The new DMN+ model improves the state of the art on both the Visual Question Answering dataset and the bAbI-10k text question-answering dataset without supporting fact supervision.","source_span":"Our new DMN+ model improves the state of the art on both the Visual Question Answering dataset and the \\babi-10k text question-answering dataset without supporting fact supervision."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The DMN yields state of the art results on question answering with supporting facts marked during training, sentiment analysis, and part-of-speech tagging.","source_span":"The DMN yields state of the art results on question answering with supporting facts marked during training, sentiment analysis, and part-of-speech tagging."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"Unlike the original DMN, the new DMN+ model does not require that supporting facts be labeled during training.","source_span":"Unlike before, the new DMN+ model does not require that supporting facts (i.e. the facts that are relevant for answering a particular question) are labeled during training."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The DMN architecture is composed of four modules: Input Module, Question Module, Episodic Memory Module, and Answer Module.","source_span":"The modules, depicted in Fig. 1 , are as follows: Input Module... Question Module... Episodic Memory Module... Answer Module"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The Input Module processes input data into a set of vectors termed facts, F=[f_1,...,f_N], where N is the total number of facts.","source_span":"This module processes the input data about which a question is being asked into a set of vectors termed facts, represented as $F=[f_1,\\hdots ,f_N]$ , where $N$ is the total number of facts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"In the original DMN, the text QA input module consists of a GRU over the input words.","source_span":"For text QA in BIBREF6 , the module consists of a GRU over the input words."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The Question Module computes the question vector q as the final hidden state of a GRU run over the words in the question.","source_span":"This module computes a vector representation $q$ of the question, where $q \\in \\mathbb {R}^{n_H}$ is the final hidden state of a GRU over the words in the question."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The initial episodic memory vector is set equal to the question vector: m^0 = q.","source_span":"the initial memory vector is set to the question vector: $m^0 = q$"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The DMN is trained using cross entropy error on the answers, which is backpropagated through the entire network.","source_span":"The cross entropy error on the answers is used for training and backpropagated through the entire network."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The new GRU formulation for the memory module incorporates attention gates computed using global knowledge over the facts.","source_span":"The new GRU formulation incorporates attention gates that are computed using global knowledge over the facts."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","statement":"The proposed new input module uses a two-level encoder with a sentence reader and an input fusion layer to allow information flow between sentences.","source_span":"We propose a new input module which uses a two level encoder with a sentence reader and input fusion layer to allow for information flow between sentences."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[1,3],"question":"How does the DMN+ model differ from the original DMN in terms of training requirements, and what state-of-the-art results does it achieve?","answer":"DMN+ does not require supporting facts to be labeled during training, and it achieves state-of-the-art results on the Visual Question Answering (VQA) dataset and the bAbI-10k text QA dataset."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What are the four modules that make up the Dynamic Memory Network architecture?","answer":"The DMN consists of an Input Module, a Question Module, an Episodic Memory Module, and an Answer Module."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[2],"question":"On what three types of tasks did the original DMN achieve state-of-the-art results when supporting facts were marked during training?","answer":"The original DMN achieved state-of-the-art results on question answering with supporting facts marked during training, sentiment analysis, and part-of-speech tagging."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[7,8],"question":"How is the question vector q computed in the DMN, and how is the initial episodic memory set?","answer":"The question vector q is the final hidden state of a GRU run over the words in the question, and the initial episode memory m^0 is set equal to q."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[9],"question":"What loss function is used to train the DMN, and how is it applied across the network?","answer":"The DMN is trained using cross entropy error on the answers, which is backpropagated through the entire network."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What new design does the paper propose for the DMN+ input module?","answer":"It proposes a two-level encoder consisting of a sentence reader and an input fusion layer, which allows information to flow between sentences."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0019","fact_refs":[6],"question":"In the original DMN, how does the input module process text for question answering?","answer":"It uses a single GRU over the input words, extracting sentence representations from the hidden states produced at the end-of-sentence markers."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The paper proposes a joint Bayesian model of unsupervised semantic role induction in multiple languages to explore the usefulness of parallel corpora for this task.","source_span":"We propose a Bayesian model of unsupervised semantic role induction in multiple languages, and use it to explore the usefulness of parallel corpora for this task."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The joint Bayesian model consists of individual models for each language plus additional latent variables that capture alignments between roles across languages.","source_span":"Our joint Bayesian model consists of individual models for each language plus additional latent variables that capture alignments between roles across languages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The biggest impact of adding a parallel corpus to training is the increase in monolingual data, with crosslingual alignments giving only small improvements even with labeled data for the other language.","source_span":"We find that the biggest impact of adding a parallel corpus to training is actually the increase in mono-lingual data, with the alignments to another language resulting in small improvements, even with labeled data for the other language."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"In the example sentence, the predicate WRITE has two arguments: 'Mike' labeled A0 (the writer) and 'a book' labeled A1 (the thing written).","source_span":"Here, the predicate WRITE has two arguments: `Mike' as A0 or the writer, and `a book' as A1 or the thing written."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The labels A0 and A1 correspond to the PropBank annotations.","source_span":"The labels A0 and A1 correspond to the PropBank annotations BIBREF0 ."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The example English-German word alignments given are Mike-Mike, written-geschrieben, and book-Buch.","source_span":"If sentences INLINEFORM0 and INLINEFORM1 have the word alignments: Mike-Mike, written-geschrieben, and book-Buch"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The paper uses the Bayesian model of garg2012unsupervised as its base monolingual model.","source_span":"We use the Bayesian model of garg2012unsupervised as our base monolingual model."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"The role inventory for each predicate is divided into Primary and Secondary roles to model role ordering and repetition preferences.","source_span":"the role inventory for each predicate is divided into Primary and Secondary roles as follows"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"garg2012unsupervised reported that in practice, Primary Roles mostly get mapped to core roles and Secondary Roles to modifier roles.","source_span":"garg2012unsupervised reported that, in practice, PRs mostly get mapped to core roles and SRs to modifier roles, which conforms to the linguistic motivations for this distinction."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"In the multilingual model, whenever the head words of arguments are aligned in parallel sentences during training, a crosslingual latent variable (CLV) is added as a parent of the two corresponding role variables.","source_span":"whenever the head words of the arguments are aligned, we add a CLV as a parent of the two corresponding role variables."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","statement":"All the multinomial and binomial distributions in the monolingual model have symmetric Dirichlet and beta priors respectively.","source_span":"All the multinomial and binomial distributions have symmetric Dirichlet and beta priors respectively."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What is the core idea behind the Bayesian model proposed for multilingual unsupervised semantic role induction?","answer":"It is a joint Bayesian model made of individual per-language models plus additional latent variables that capture role alignments across languages, used to study how useful parallel corpora are for semantic role induction."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[2],"question":"According to the paper's findings, what actually drives the biggest improvement when a parallel corpus is added to training?","answer":"The biggest improvement comes from the increase in monolingual data itself, not from the crosslingual alignments—those give only small gains even when labeled data is available for the other language."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[3,4],"question":"In the example sentence with the predicate WRITE, what roles are assigned to 'Mike' and 'a book', and what annotation scheme do these labels come from?","answer":"'Mike' is labeled A0 (the writer) and 'a book' is labeled A1 (the thing written), following the PropBank annotation scheme."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What word alignments are given between the English and German example sentences?","answer":"The word alignments given are Mike-Mike, written-geschrieben, and book-Buch."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Which base monolingual model does this paper build on, and how is the role inventory structured in it?","answer":"It builds on the Bayesian model of garg2012unsupervised for unsupervised SRL, in which each predicate's role inventory is divided into Primary Roles (PRs) and Secondary Roles (SRs) to capture ordering and repetition preferences."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[9],"question":"How does the multilingual model incorporate crosslingual information when training on parallel sentences?","answer":"Whenever the head words of arguments are aligned across a parallel sentence pair, the model adds a crosslingual latent variable (CLV) as a parent of the two corresponding role variables in each language's model."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0020","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What did garg2012unsupervised find about how Primary Roles and Secondary Roles map onto gold roles in practice?","answer":"Primary Roles mostly map to core roles, while Secondary Roles mostly map to modifier roles, consistent with the linguistic motivation for the distinction."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"The paper proposes leveraging domain-specific features (specific vocabulary usage, specific codes, and document type) to improve automatic quality evaluation of Wikipedia medical articles.","source_span":"In this paper, we propose to leverage specific domain features to improve the results of the evaluation of Wikipedia medical articles."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"The evaluation adopts an 'actionable' model whose features relate to article content, so the model can directly suggest strategies for improving a given article's quality.","source_span":"we evaluate the articles adopting an \"actionable\" model, whose features are related to the content of the articles, so that the model can also directly suggest strategies for improving a given article quality."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"The approach was validated by classifying medical articles from the Wikipedia Medicine Portal that had been previously manually labeled by the Wiki Project team.","source_span":"We prove the effectiveness of our approach by classifying the medical articles of the Wikipedia Medicine Portal, which have been previously manually labeled by the Wiki Project team."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"According to the 2014 Eurobarometer survey on European citizens' digital health literacy, around six out of ten respondents have used the Internet to search for health-related information.","source_span":"As pointed out by the 2014 Eurobarometer survey on European citizens' digital health literacy, around six out of ten respondents have used the Internet to search for health-related information."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"The literature has traditionally adopted the 'Flesch-Kincaid' measure to automatically assess readability in textual documents.","source_span":"the literature has widely adopted well known criteria, as the \u201cFlesch-Kincaid\" measure\" BIBREF2 , to automatically assess readability in textual documents."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"The paper's baseline 'actionable model' comes from prior work (BIBREF7) that uses classification techniques based on structural and linguistic features of an article.","source_span":"the work in BIBREF7 exploits classification techniques based on structural and linguistic features of an article."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"Because Wikipedia can be edited by anyone regardless of expertise, this tends to erode the average reputation of its sources and the trustworthiness of the content.","source_span":"since Wikipedia can be edited by anyone, regardless of their level of expertise, this tends to erode the average reputation of the sources, and, consequently, the trustworthiness of the contents posted by those sources."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","statement":"There has been a reported case of a blackmail network of accounts that threatened celebrities by menacing to insert offending information on their Wikipedia pages.","source_span":"the recent news on the uncovering of a blackmail network of accounts, which threatened celebrities with the menace of inserting offending information on their Wikipedia pages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What kind of features does this paper propose adding to improve automatic quality evaluation of Wikipedia medical articles, and what makes the resulting model 'actionable'?","answer":"The paper proposes adding domain-specific features (such as specific vocabulary usage, specific codes, and document type) to an 'actionable' model whose features are tied to article content, letting it directly suggest strategies to improve a given article's quality."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[2],"question":"How did the authors validate the effectiveness of their approach?","answer":"They classified medical articles from the Wikipedia Medicine Portal that had previously been manually labeled by the Wiki Project team."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[3],"question":"According to the 2014 Eurobarometer survey cited in the paper, what proportion of European respondents had used the Internet to search for health-related information?","answer":"Around six out of ten respondents had used the Internet to search for health-related information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What well-known measure has traditionally been used in the literature to automatically assess the readability of text documents?","answer":"The 'Flesch-Kincaid' measure."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What baseline model does this paper build on for the article quality evaluation task, and what does that baseline use?","answer":"The baseline is the 'actionable model' from prior work (BIBREF7), which uses classification techniques based on structural and linguistic features of an article."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why does the paper say Wikipedia's open editing model can hurt trust in its content?","answer":"Because anyone can edit Wikipedia regardless of expertise, which tends to erode the average reputation of its sources and, consequently, the trustworthiness of the content they contribute."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0021","fact_refs":[7],"question":"What malicious activity involving Wikipedia accounts does the paper mention as a recent concern?","answer":"It mentions the uncovering of a blackmail network of accounts that threatened celebrities by menacing to insert offending information on their Wikipedia pages."}
